        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">A chest strap is the more dependable heart rate sensor on a
            treadmill; a wrist watch is usually fine for steady, easy sessions.</strong> The strap
            reads each heartbeat's electrical signal, while the watch infers it from blood flow and
            can lag in intervals or lock onto your stride.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Use a strap for intervals and HRV, an arm band if a strap is uncomfortable, your watch for
            steady easy work, and the handrail grips only as a spot check at a walk. For straps and
            arm bands that work with consoles, watches and apps, see our
